### What is the difference between TreeMap and HashMap?

| Feature | HashMap | TreeMap |
|---|---|---|
| Internal structure | Hash table (Node[]) | Red-Black tree |
| get/put | O(1) average | O(log n) |
| Ordering | None | Sorted by key |
| Null key | 1 allowed | Not allowed (NPE) |
| Null values | Yes | Yes |
| Equality | hashCode + equals | compareTo or Comparator |
| NavigableMap | No | Yes |
| Memory per entry | ~32 bytes | ~48 bytes |
| Thread-safe | No | No |
| Concurrent equivalent | ConcurrentHashMap | ConcurrentSkipListMap |

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.*;

public class TreeMapVsHashMapDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // HashMap: fast, unordered
        Map<String, BigDecimal> hashMap = new HashMap<>();
        hashMap.put("SAV2001", new BigDecimal("150000"));
        hashMap.put("CUR3001", new BigDecimal("75000"));
        hashMap.put("FD4001", new BigDecimal("500000"));
        System.out.println("HashMap: " + hashMap.keySet());

        // TreeMap: sorted by key
        TreeMap<String, BigDecimal> treeMap = new TreeMap<>(hashMap);
        System.out.println("TreeMap: " + treeMap.keySet());

        // TreeMap range query
        System.out.println("Range [CUR..SAV): " + treeMap.subMap("CUR3001", "SAV2001"));
        System.out.println("Floor of 'G': " + treeMap.floorKey("G"));
    }
}
```

```text
HashMap: [FD4001, CUR3001, SAV2001]
TreeMap: [CUR3001, FD4001, SAV2001]
Range [CUR..SAV): {CUR3001=75000, FD4001=500000}
Floor of 'G': FD4001
```

### When should you use TreeMap?

Use TreeMap when:
- You need keys in sorted order for reports or display.
- You need range queries: "all accounts between CUR and SAV."
- You need first/last key without scanning all entries.
- You need floor/ceiling/lower/higher lookups.

Use HashMap when:
- You only need fast key-value lookup.
- Order does not matter.
- You want the best possible performance.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.util.*;

public class TreeMapUseCaseDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Sorted map of transactions by date
        TreeMap<LocalDate, BigDecimal> dailyTotals = new TreeMap<>();
        dailyTotals.put(LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 15), new BigDecimal("5000000"));
        dailyTotals.put(LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 16), new BigDecimal("3500000"));
        dailyTotals.put(LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 17), new BigDecimal("7200000"));
        dailyTotals.put(LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 18), new BigDecimal("4100000"));
        dailyTotals.put(LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 19), new BigDecimal("6800000"));

        // Range query: transactions from Jan 16 to Jan 18 (inclusive)
        var range = dailyTotals.subMap(
            LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 16), true,
            LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 18), true
        );
        System.out.println("Jan 16-18: " + range);

        // Most recent day
        System.out.println("Latest: " + dailyTotals.lastEntry());
    }
}
```

```text
Jan 16-18: {2024-01-16=3500000, 2024-01-17=7200000, 2024-01-18=4100000}
Latest: 2024-01-19=6800000
```

### What are common mistakes?

#### Mistake: Using TreeMap for fast lookups when order is not needed

```java
// WRONG: O(log n) per lookup when O(1) is available
Map<String, String> lookup = new TreeMap<>(); // unnecessary sorting overhead

// CORRECT: use HashMap when order is irrelevant
Map<String, String> lookup = new HashMap<>(); // O(1) lookups
```

#### Mistake: TreeMap with keys that have inconsistent compareTo and equals

```java
// TreeMap uses compareTo for equality, not equals!
// If compareTo returns 0 but equals returns false:
// TreeMap considers them the SAME key (overwrites)
// HashMap considers them DIFFERENT keys
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

- **TreeMap is a NavigableMap.** Always declare as `NavigableMap<K,V>` or `SortedMap<K,V>` when using TreeMap-specific methods, to make the API contract clear.

- **Performance gap.** For 1 million entries, HashMap.get is about 5-10x faster than TreeMap.get. This difference matters in high-throughput banking systems.

- **ConcurrentSkipListMap.** The thread-safe sorted map. Uses a skip list (probabilistic balanced structure) instead of a Red-Black tree. Supports NavigableMap.

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "When would you use TreeMap over HashMap?" – When you need sorted keys or range queries.
- "What is the internal structure of TreeMap?" – Red-Black tree (self-balancing BST).
- "Why can't TreeMap have null keys?" – compareTo throws NPE on null.
- "What is the time complexity of TreeMap.get?" – O(log n).
